[1m[95m[NARRATION][0m 
  You reach into your pack, fingers brushing past rations and tinder, finding 
  the familiar cold shape of the compass. You pull it free — a palm-sized disc 
  of brass, its needle wobbling as it catches the lamplight. Mara's eyes track 
  the motion. She leans back, just slightly. "Hold." Her voice is quiet but 
  cuts clean through the tavern's murmur. She doesn't reach for it. "Before you 
  hand that over — you sure? That compass has been in your family, hasn't it? I 
  saw how you held it when we first met. Like it was the last solid thing in 
  the world." She lets the silence stretch. A log shifts in the hearth. The 
  barmaid scrapes a plate. "I'm not saying I won't take it. I'm saying — once 
  it's in my coat, it's not in your hand. And if I go through that gate, it 
  goes with me." She watches you. Waiting. The rain drums a steady rhythm 
  against the glass. "So. You handing it over, or are we still talking?" 

