[1m[95m[NARRATION][0m 
  The compass hangs between you, brass warm from your palm. Mara watches your 
  hand. Reads your face. Something shifts behind her eyes — a decision made. 
  She pulls off her left glove. A scar cuts across the back of her hand — a 
  crescent, pale and puckered, still healing. The kind of wound a blade leaves 
  when it's *pulling* rather than striking. "Three nights ago." Her voice 
  drops. "I was at the Ash Gate. Midnight. I wanted to see if the lock really 
  held." She flexes her fingers. The scar tissue pulls tight. "It didn't." She 
  puts the glove back on, slow and deliberate. "Something on the other side 
  reached through. Not a hand. Not claws. Something cold — like frostbite with 
  intent. It grabbed the lock from inside and *twisted*. The bronze groaned. 
  The gate shifted — maybe an inch." She looks up. "I grabbed the bar to hold 
  it shut. That's where I got this." A pause. The rain fills the silence. "I've 
  been asking questions ever since. Quiet ones. The kind that get you watched. 
  Nobody in the Watch will touch this. The merchant guild pays them not to. And 
  the people who know what's really behind that gate — they're either dead or 
  wearing masks." She holds out her hand, palm open. "So yes. I'm in. Not as a 
  hired blade or a curious scholar. As your ally. Whatever's behind that gate, 
  whoever's feeding it — we find them together." Her eyes don't waver. "Compass 
  or no compass. Deal?" 

