  Mara reaches into an inner pocket of her charcoal coat — not the one she 
  stowed the gold in. Her fingers emerge pinching a thin chain. Dangling from 
  it: a key, carved from polished obsidian. Black as still water. No shine, no 
  reflection — it drinks the lamplight instead. She sets it on the table 
  between you. The metal *clinks* against the wood. The sound is heavier than 
  something that size should make. "I found this in the lock of the Ash Gate. 
  After I pulled my hand back." She doesn't touch it again. "I've had it three 
  days. It's cold. Always cold. Doesn't warm up in my pocket. Doesn't warm up 
  in my hand." She watches you. The key lies on the scarred oak. A single ridge 
  runs its length. The obsidian is smooth — too smooth, like water frozen 
  mid-pour. The chain is plain iron, rusted at the links. The barmaid glances 
  over. Sees the key. Looks away fast. Crosses herself. Mara's voice is low. 
  "That's the only lead I've got on what's *pushing* from the other side. 
  Someone made this. Someone put it in the lock. And whoever they are — they 
  wanted that gate open." She nods at it. "Your call. You want to hold onto it, 
  or leave it here?" 
