[1m[95m[NARRATION][0m 
  You stand in the middle of the Copper Finch. The reliquary hisses faintly. 
  The barmaid is frozen. The rain drums against the shutters. You call out: 
  *"Old Bram."* The name lands flat in the tavern air. No echo. No answer. Just 
  the wet creak of the sign outside, and the drip of water through a loose 
  shingle somewhere overhead. The barmaid flinches. Her lips move — maybe a 
  prayer, maybe a curse. You didn't expect an answer. You knew the ash ferryman 
  is dead. You saw the truth of it in the silence around his name, in the way 
  the city guards avoid the gate he used to watch. But you called anyway — a 
  habit, a test, a thread pulled to see what comes loose. And in the quiet that 
  follows, his voice comes back to you. Not from the room. From memory. That 
  last night at the Ash Gate, the rain just like this, Bram's hand on your arm 
  — leathery grip, older than the stones he stood on. *"Don't open it. Don't 
  touch it. Don't even look at it too long, lad. Some locks ain't meant to be 
  turned. Some doors ain't doors at all — they're mouths. And that one's 
  hungry."* He'd let go of your arm. Spat into the gutter. Walked away into the 
  dark. Two days later, they found him face-down on his own ferry, the tide 
  lapping at his boots. The Watch called it drink. The water called it 
  something else. The memory fades. The reliquary hisses again — a wetter sound 
  now. The barmaid finds her voice, small and tight: "The back door's still 
  open. If you're going — go." 

