[1m[95m[NARRATION][0m 
  You step through the back door into the alley. Rain hits your face 
  immediately — cold, relentless, the kind of morning rain that seeps into 
  collars and stays there. The Copper Finch's kitchen door swings shut behind 
  you, cutting off the lamplight. The alley empties onto a narrow street. Mud 
  underfoot. Shuttered windows. A dog watches from a doorway, doesn't bark. You 
  walk east. Two streets. That's what Mara said. Two streets from the Ash Gate. 
  You pass a chandlery with a black candle sign — blue flame painted on the 
  wood — and you note it without stopping. Sable Tallow's shop. Later. Not now. 
  The street dead-ends at the gate. It rises out of the city wall like a 
  cracked tooth — black stone, veined with pale lines where age and pressure 
  have split the arch. The gate itself is iron, banded, fitted into the stone 
  with bolts the size of a child's fist. No portcullis. No guard post. Just the 
  arch, the iron, and the silence. The rain doesn't fall *through* the arch. It 
  streams around it, as if the air under the curve is denser. Heavier. The 
  ground beneath it is dry — a perfect crescent of dust and scattered ash, 
  untouched by water. The iron gate is closed. The lock is visible from here — 
  a mechanism set into the gate's face, black iron, ornate, with a keyhole that 
  seems deeper than the metal it's cut into. No one else is here. No Watch 
  patrol. No early risers. Just the rain, the ash, and the gate. The obsidian 
  key weighs against your pack — not physically, but the memory of its cold. 
  The cold that drank the lamplight. The arch looms. The lock waits. 

